1
00:00:00,003 --> 00:00:01,157
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:01,177 --> 00:00:03,288
In this series, I'll take you from zero

3
00:00:03,308 --> 00:00:04,880
to getting started with Claude Code.

4
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,953
I'm sure those of you who clicked

5
00:00:06,973 --> 00:00:09,250
in already have some experience using AI,

6
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:11,419
and this video will walk you through

7
00:00:11,439 --> 00:00:13,410
how to use AI, systematically.

8
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:15,799
AI is a tool, and you can only use a tool well

9
00:00:15,819 --> 00:00:17,613
once you know how to use it.

10
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:19,286
All the material here comes

11
00:00:19,306 --> 00:00:21,913
from the free resources on Anthropic's website,

12
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:23,361
and you can find the original

13
00:00:23,381 --> 00:00:25,923
content the way you see on screen.

14
00:00:26,303 --> 00:00:27,013
Alright, let's begin.

15
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:27,769
First concept:

16
00:00:27,789 --> 00:00:29,645
Claude Code and Claude Cowork.

17
00:00:29,665 --> 00:00:30,940
From here on I'll just call them Claude.

18
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:32,630
What's the difference between these two?

19
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:34,436
The Claude in the web

20
00:00:34,456 --> 00:00:36,670
chat window is a chatbox.

21
00:00:36,690 --> 00:00:37,808
It can answer questions,

22
00:00:37,828 --> 00:00:40,084
but it can't modify the files on your computer.

23
00:00:40,104 --> 00:00:42,723
It can't reach its hands into your machine.

24
00:00:42,743 --> 00:00:44,008
After it writes code,

25
00:00:44,028 --> 00:00:46,613
I have to copy, paste and run it myself.

26
00:00:46,633 --> 00:00:47,562
If the code has an error,

27
00:00:47,582 --> 00:00:49,013
I have to paste it back and ask again.

28
00:00:49,033 --> 00:00:51,021
That's what AI looked like a few years ago.

29
00:00:51,041 --> 00:00:52,915
Claude Code puts

30
00:00:52,935 --> 00:00:55,170
that hand into my computer's operating system.

31
00:00:55,190 --> 00:00:56,918
It can create, modify

32
00:00:56,938 --> 00:00:59,813
and delete anything on the whole computer, any file.

33
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:02,427
So what is Claude Code?

34
00:01:02,447 --> 00:01:04,205
Claude Code is an AI assistant

35
00:01:04,225 --> 00:01:05,513
made by Anthropic.

36
00:01:05,533 --> 00:01:08,303
It works on your computer and does the work itself.

37
00:01:08,323 --> 00:01:09,929
Take an example:

38
00:01:09,949 --> 00:01:12,694
a chat window is like hiring a consultant

39
00:01:12,714 --> 00:01:14,760
who gives me advice over the phone.

40
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:16,782
It answers the questions I ask,

41
00:01:16,802 --> 00:01:18,227
but I have to paste things in.

42
00:01:18,247 --> 00:01:19,403
If there's an error,

43
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:21,813
I have to paste it into that window and ask.

44
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:23,188
Claude Code is like hiring

45
00:01:23,208 --> 00:01:25,574
an assistant, a colleague,

46
00:01:25,594 --> 00:01:27,730
who sits right at your computer

47
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,131
and finds code in your project files, edits code,

48
00:01:30,151 --> 00:01:32,532
runs commands, searches the whole project,

49
00:01:32,552 --> 00:01:34,213
creates and deletes files.

50
00:01:34,233 --> 00:01:35,423
By default it only works

51
00:01:35,443 --> 00:01:38,287
inside this folder and its subfolders.

52
00:01:38,307 --> 00:01:40,361
If it wants to touch anywhere else,

53
00:01:40,381 --> 00:01:42,333
it needs your permission.

54
00:01:42,353 --> 00:01:45,063
So how do I start Claude?

55
00:01:45,083 --> 00:01:47,398
You type the claude command in a terminal,

56
00:01:47,418 --> 00:01:49,173
and it starts.

57
00:01:49,193 --> 00:01:50,236
What's a terminal?

58
00:01:50,256 --> 00:01:52,362
It's the window where you can only type commands,

59
00:01:52,382 --> 00:01:54,207
the one with black text on a white background.

60
00:01:54,227 --> 00:01:56,367
Some of you may find that unfamiliar,

61
00:01:56,387 --> 00:01:58,527
maybe even a bit off-putting,

62
00:01:58,547 --> 00:02:00,374
but don't worry.

63
00:02:00,394 --> 00:02:02,912
Once you've typed the claude command,

64
00:02:02,932 --> 00:02:05,223
you just talk to it in plain language.

65
00:02:05,243 --> 00:02:07,393
Software that can see its environment,

66
00:02:07,413 --> 00:02:09,562
take action and reach a goal step by step

67
00:02:09,582 --> 00:02:12,703
is called an agent in the industry. In English, an AI Agent.

68
00:02:12,723 --> 00:02:13,513
Claude Code

69
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:15,584
is a typical agent framework.

70
00:02:15,604 --> 00:02:16,937
It has "Code" in the name,

71
00:02:16,957 --> 00:02:19,124
but it isn't only for writing code.

72
00:02:19,144 --> 00:02:21,016
It can write documents, run programs,

73
00:02:21,036 --> 00:02:23,338
search files, look things up, all of it.

74
00:02:23,358 --> 00:02:26,334
So its real skill is doing the work for you.

75
00:02:26,354 --> 00:02:28,488
OK, now that we know what it is,

76
00:02:28,508 --> 00:02:30,288
what can it actually do?

77
00:02:30,308 --> 00:02:32,534
First, it can read a project.

78
00:02:32,554 --> 00:02:33,664
For example,

79
00:02:33,684 --> 00:02:34,920
ask it what this project does.

80
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:36,804
It goes through the files itself and explains it to you.

81
00:02:36,824 --> 00:02:38,941
You can also ask for more detail,

82
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:40,224
or a shorter version,

83
00:02:40,244 --> 00:02:42,761
or an explanation in plain language.

84
00:02:42,781 --> 00:02:44,274
Second, it edits code.

85
00:02:44,294 --> 00:02:46,524
The user states the effect and requirements they want,

86
00:02:46,544 --> 00:02:49,304
say, a really cool web page,

87
00:02:49,324 --> 00:02:51,892
and it takes your request as its goal,

88
00:02:51,912 --> 00:02:53,718
finds animation libraries online by itself,

89
00:02:53,738 --> 00:02:55,240
finds the local files itself,

90
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:56,788
and gives you the finished edit.

91
00:02:56,808 --> 00:02:59,406
Third, it runs commands: install a program,

92
00:02:59,426 --> 00:03:01,408
uninstall one, run a program,

93
00:03:01,428 --> 00:03:04,101
run a check, whatever result you want to see.

94
00:03:04,121 --> 00:03:07,041
It runs those commands and then gives you the answer.

95
00:03:07,061 --> 00:03:08,940
Fourth, it looks things up online.

96
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:12,061
For example, I make news-roundup content,

97
00:03:12,081 --> 00:03:14,482
and it searches the news at a set time every day,

98
00:03:14,502 --> 00:03:15,621
organizes it,

99
00:03:15,641 --> 00:03:17,814
and creates a new document for me to read.

100
00:03:17,834 --> 00:03:20,397
You don't need to direct these one by one.

101
00:03:20,417 --> 00:03:22,291
Write it up in advance,

102
00:03:22,311 --> 00:03:24,185
tell it the goal and what to do first and next,

103
00:03:24,205 --> 00:03:26,794
and it does it by itself.

104
00:03:26,814 --> 00:03:30,074
So how does it do its work?

105
00:03:30,094 --> 00:03:32,308
This is where a loop comes in.

106
00:03:32,328 --> 00:03:35,528
This multi-round process is called the agent loop.

107
00:03:35,548 --> 00:03:38,281
It takes your request, your prompt,

108
00:03:38,301 --> 00:03:40,424
as the goal it must complete,

109
00:03:40,444 --> 00:03:42,745
then goes through several rounds of thinking

110
00:03:42,765 --> 00:03:44,174
and acting to reach it.

111
00:03:44,194 --> 00:03:47,014
That's what people call the agentic loop.

112
00:03:47,034 --> 00:03:49,470
Let's take an example. Say you tell it:

113
00:03:49,490 --> 00:03:50,699
I built a web page,

114
00:03:50,719 --> 00:03:53,155
the login page doesn't respond when I click,

115
00:03:53,175 --> 00:03:55,621
please fix it, and you send that.

116
00:03:55,641 --> 00:03:57,424
When Claude receives your request,

117
00:03:57,444 --> 00:03:59,601
it lists it as a goal it must complete.

118
00:03:59,621 --> 00:04:01,275
Then Claude uses the command line,

119
00:04:01,295 --> 00:04:03,581
which is its tool, to look at the code files.

120
00:04:03,601 --> 00:04:05,246
You don't need to worry about any of this;

121
00:04:05,266 --> 00:04:07,324
it's just for understanding.

122
00:04:07,344 --> 00:04:09,314
Claude does these things by itself;

123
00:04:09,334 --> 00:04:11,531
we only need to understand them.

124
00:04:11,551 --> 00:04:13,183
Claude uses its commands

125
00:04:13,203 --> 00:04:15,100
to look at the code files,

126
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:17,888
finds the code related to the login page,

127
00:04:17,908 --> 00:04:21,421
reads the error, and then goes to edit it.

128
00:04:21,491 --> 00:04:23,127
It works out where the problem is,

129
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:25,191
edits the file, and then runs it.

130
00:04:25,211 --> 00:04:26,691
After running it, it checks whether it's fixed.

131
00:04:26,711 --> 00:04:28,691
If it is, it has reached the goal.

132
00:04:28,711 --> 00:04:31,335
If it isn't, go back to step one,

133
00:04:31,355 --> 00:04:32,987
edit the code again, go around once more,

134
00:04:33,007 --> 00:04:35,911
and keep going until the goal is met.

135
00:04:35,931 --> 00:04:38,020
These three steps aren't a rigid assembly line;

136
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:39,324
they blend together.

137
00:04:39,344 --> 00:04:41,032
Sometimes you ask one question

138
00:04:41,052 --> 00:04:42,584
and it only needs half a loop.

139
00:04:42,604 --> 00:04:44,140
If it finishes a task in one step,

140
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:46,644
there's no loop at all.

141
00:04:46,664 --> 00:04:48,690
If a problem is harder,

142
00:04:48,710 --> 00:04:50,906
it usually edits over and over and runs

143
00:04:50,926 --> 00:04:52,901
commands over and over.

144
00:04:52,921 --> 00:04:54,271
It can chain dozens of steps,

145
00:04:54,291 --> 00:04:56,504
correcting itself as it goes.

146
00:04:56,524 --> 00:04:57,783
One more key point:

147
00:04:57,803 --> 00:05:00,199
if you see it getting something wrong,

148
00:05:00,219 --> 00:05:01,621
or you have a new idea,

149
00:05:01,641 --> 00:05:03,694
you can interrupt it at any time.

150
00:05:03,714 --> 00:05:05,874
Press ESC to make it stop.

151
00:05:05,894 --> 00:05:08,328
Or if you want to add something

152
00:05:08,348 --> 00:05:11,109
of your own into its loop,

153
00:05:11,129 --> 00:05:12,745
type it out and press Enter

154
00:05:12,765 --> 00:05:14,364
to send it.

155
00:05:14,384 --> 00:05:16,911
When it finishes the batch it's working on,

156
00:05:16,931 --> 00:05:19,991
it reads what you wrote and adjusts.

157
00:05:20,011 --> 00:05:21,846
Fourth point: how

158
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:24,928
does this loop actually run?

159
00:05:24,948 --> 00:05:26,688
That brings us to four things.

160
00:05:26,708 --> 00:05:28,947
First, the brain. Second, the hands.

161
00:05:28,967 --> 00:05:31,814
Third, its memory. Fourth, the key.

162
00:05:31,834 --> 00:05:32,780
Start with the brain:

163
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,754
its brain is the Claude model.

164
00:05:34,774 --> 00:05:37,094
The Claude model isn't local; it's in the cloud.

165
00:05:37,114 --> 00:05:39,228
The model understands what you say,

166
00:05:39,248 --> 00:05:41,524
reads code, and decides what to do next,

167
00:05:41,544 --> 00:05:42,844
but it can't carry out these tasks

168
00:05:42,864 --> 00:05:44,734
or run these commands locally.

169
00:05:44,834 --> 00:05:46,664
Its hands, meaning its tools,

170
00:05:46,684 --> 00:05:48,498
are what run those commands.

171
00:05:48,518 --> 00:05:50,485
The hands don't know what to run,

172
00:05:50,505 --> 00:05:51,905
so the brain tells the hands

173
00:05:51,925 --> 00:05:54,176
which commands to execute,

174
00:05:54,196 --> 00:05:56,184
and only then can it change local content.

175
00:05:56,204 --> 00:05:58,376
Say you have some code locally

176
00:05:58,396 --> 00:06:01,584
and you ask it to fix something.

177
00:06:01,604 --> 00:06:02,700
Your prompt is sent

178
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,124
to the Claude model in the cloud.

179
00:06:05,144 --> 00:06:06,731
The model says: I need to find

180
00:06:06,751 --> 00:06:09,639
which files this bug is in.

181
00:06:09,659 --> 00:06:11,333
It sends that to your machine,

182
00:06:11,353 --> 00:06:13,574
so Claude Code uses tools

183
00:06:13,594 --> 00:06:16,104
to look at the code containing the bug.

184
00:06:16,124 --> 00:06:18,469
Then your request plus that code goes back

185
00:06:18,489 --> 00:06:21,044
to the Claude model, the brain.

186
00:06:21,064 --> 00:06:23,724
After the brain decides how to change the code,

187
00:06:23,744 --> 00:06:25,970
the local side calls the tools,

188
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:27,513
edits the code and runs it. If it passes,

189
00:06:27,533 --> 00:06:29,338
that's sent back to the brain,

190
00:06:29,358 --> 00:06:31,134
which says: OK, the task is done.

191
00:06:31,154 --> 00:06:32,950
It sends the completed result back

192
00:06:32,970 --> 00:06:33,728
to your machine,

193
00:06:33,748 --> 00:06:36,144
and that's how you see the outcome.

194
00:06:36,164 --> 00:06:37,644
The third thing is memory,

195
00:06:37,664 --> 00:06:39,444
formally called the context window.

196
00:06:39,464 --> 00:06:41,760
The prompts you wrote, the files it read,

197
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:43,412
and the results of the commands it ran

198
00:06:43,432 --> 00:06:44,884
are all in its memory.

199
00:06:44,904 --> 00:06:47,387
But a model's memory is limited,

200
00:06:47,407 --> 00:06:50,114
just like storage, and when it fills up,

201
00:06:50,134 --> 00:06:52,012
the model automatically compresses,

202
00:06:52,032 --> 00:06:54,484
and automatically clears out old commands.

203
00:06:54,504 --> 00:06:56,174
The cost is that details

204
00:06:56,194 --> 00:06:57,865
you gave long ago are gone.

205
00:06:57,885 --> 00:07:00,062
For instance, what you sent at the very beginning,

206
00:07:00,082 --> 00:07:02,444
it may no longer know.

207
00:07:02,464 --> 00:07:04,048
And every time you open

208
00:07:04,068 --> 00:07:05,518
a new conversation,

209
00:07:05,538 --> 00:07:07,291
its memory is empty;

210
00:07:07,361 --> 00:07:09,813
it won't carry over the chat from another window;

211
00:07:09,833 --> 00:07:11,528
they're all independent.

212
00:07:11,548 --> 00:07:13,071
If you want it to follow

213
00:07:13,091 --> 00:07:14,474
a rule every time,

214
00:07:14,494 --> 00:07:16,981
write it into a file called CLAUDE.md.

215
00:07:17,001 --> 00:07:19,247
Every time it starts a new session,

216
00:07:19,267 --> 00:07:21,066
a new conversation,

217
00:07:21,086 --> 00:07:22,701
it reads that file first.

218
00:07:22,721 --> 00:07:24,732
That file is important; a later video

219
00:07:24,752 --> 00:07:26,508
will cover it in detail.

220
00:07:26,528 --> 00:07:28,921
The fourth is the key, which means permissions.

221
00:07:28,941 --> 00:07:30,360
When Claude Code wants

222
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:32,135
to change your local files,

223
00:07:32,155 --> 00:07:34,068
you can set permissions:

224
00:07:34,088 --> 00:07:35,988
what it may change and what it may not.

225
00:07:36,008 --> 00:07:38,266
For example, there's manual mode,

226
00:07:38,286 --> 00:07:41,169
where it asks you first whenever it changes a file.

227
00:07:41,189 --> 00:07:42,613
Then there's accept-edits mode,

228
00:07:42,633 --> 00:07:45,415
where it edits files in the project folder without asking you,

229
00:07:45,435 --> 00:07:46,697
and doesn't ask about common file operations

230
00:07:46,717 --> 00:07:49,322
like creating a folder, moving, or deleting either;

231
00:07:49,342 --> 00:07:50,462
other commands it still asks about.

232
00:07:50,482 --> 00:07:51,851
Plan mode only works out a plan

233
00:07:51,871 --> 00:07:54,282
and doesn't touch your code.

234
00:07:54,302 --> 00:07:56,559
Auto lets it go ahead on its own,

235
00:07:56,579 --> 00:07:58,485
with a safety check in the background

236
00:07:58,505 --> 00:08:01,045
that blocks dangerous operations like deletions.

237
00:08:01,065 --> 00:08:03,174
And there's a mode

238
00:08:03,194 --> 00:08:05,728
that turns off all of that asking,

239
00:08:05,748 --> 00:08:08,825
which means everything is allowed,

240
00:08:08,845 --> 00:08:10,655
but I don't recommend beginners turn it on.

241
00:08:10,675 --> 00:08:13,064
With these four things, the brain,

242
00:08:13,084 --> 00:08:16,015
the hands, the memory and the permission key,

243
00:08:16,035 --> 00:08:18,687
it becomes a local agent

244
00:08:18,707 --> 00:08:20,189
that can complete your task

245
00:08:20,209 --> 00:08:22,175
the way you ask.

246
00:08:22,195 --> 00:08:24,712
We've covered the good side,

247
00:08:24,732 --> 00:08:26,165
so what's the bad side?

248
00:08:26,185 --> 00:08:28,425
This is a bit of a cold shower.

249
00:08:28,445 --> 00:08:31,239
It can hallucinate, it's a probabilistic model,

250
00:08:31,259 --> 00:08:34,709
and it can have bugs.

251
00:08:34,729 --> 00:08:36,260
As models get stronger there

252
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:38,769
are fewer and fewer bugs, but still,

253
00:08:38,789 --> 00:08:39,834
on social media,

254
00:08:39,854 --> 00:08:41,609
sometimes a friend posts something

255
00:08:41,629 --> 00:08:42,792
like: oh no,

256
00:08:42,812 --> 00:08:44,889
it deleted one of my files.

257
00:08:44,909 --> 00:08:46,598
So there's still some risk.

258
00:08:46,618 --> 00:08:48,450
Be careful with permission commands day to day,

259
00:08:48,470 --> 00:08:50,585
and don't turn it all the way up.

260
00:08:50,605 --> 00:08:52,869
Finally, how do you get to use it?

261
00:08:52,889 --> 00:08:54,058
I mentioned one way already:

262
00:08:54,078 --> 00:08:55,010
use it in the terminal.

263
00:08:55,030 --> 00:08:57,006
Type the claude command and press Enter;

264
00:08:57,026 --> 00:08:58,314
it installs and starts,

265
00:08:58,334 --> 00:09:00,469
then you log into your account and you can use it.

266
00:09:00,489 --> 00:09:02,152
It also has a plugin

267
00:09:02,172 --> 00:09:04,222
for your code editor,

268
00:09:04,242 --> 00:09:05,646
a desktop app,

269
00:09:05,666 --> 00:09:07,769
and a web version.

270
00:09:08,189 --> 00:09:10,047
OK, that's it for this beginner's

271
00:09:10,067 --> 00:09:11,515
video on Claude.

272
00:09:11,535 --> 00:09:14,365
I have two suggestions. First, when you use it,

